A||||6||gmata||Instumental Cluster Replacement||||11||
Z||000000||gmata||06-08-2000||11:47 AM||||It seems that the previous owner had installed an instrumental panel for a 4 cyclinder engine.  The problem is that the engine is a 6 cylinder!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>How hard is it to replace the cluster?  I found one online, but the dealership is going to charge me $100 to install it.  Is there a website that might contain step-by-step instructions?||||reg||11
Z||000001||Carrolles||06-08-2000||01:27 PM||||There are about 8 or 10 screws to remove the shroud and the cluster from the panel and 2 or 3 wiring plugs to simply unplug and plug in the new cluster.  Don't pay someone else to do it.  If someone else doesn't give you a web address to refer to I can scan in the diagrams from my shop manual and send them to you.<P>If you have a v-6 without the aux. gauges you will need the matching cluster unless you can get the aux. gauges with it.  The cluster with the aux. set has the oil press and volt meter in the aux gauge set above tha radio.  The other clusters have the oil press gauge beneath the tach. and no volt meter.<P>Just curious.  What indications do you have that your cluster is from a 4 cyl car?  I believe the 4 cyl and 6 cyl clusters without the aux. gauges are pretty similar.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||06-08-2000||01:39 PM||||it's a piece of cake!<P>1. remove plastic covers<P>2. disconnect wiring harnesses. horizontal and vertical connectors. don't forget the one underneath the guages.<P>3. loosen 10mm bolts that hold the guages in<P>4. check for any other screws in front or underneath.<BR>5. take them out.<P>hope this helps!!||||reg||12
Z||000003||tgowens||06-08-2000||01:50 PM||||he's right - piece of cake -- you will need a small torx bit and a 7mm socket and a 10mm socket -- there are three torx screws on the top of the rear housing, two 10mm (i think) hex head screws on each side of the housing, two on the rear of the guage panel (holding it to the dashboard) and two underneath the bottom panel as well as two 7mm hex head screws on the bottom panel. There is a large wiring harness on the rear of each side of the guage panel and one underneath the left front as well as a small wiring plug on the right rear............that's basically it.....I certainly wouldn't pay someone $100 to do the job -- you can do it easily!<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 06-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||gmata||06-08-2000||03:01 PM||||Thanks for the information.  I guess I will order the cluster and install it myself.<P>The reason I was suspious was that the tach was riding high.  It was registering 5000 rpms at 60 mph.  I took the car to the dealership and they check the cluster and everything was fine.  The engine sounds normal even though the tach is registering high rpms.  Since I just bought the car from the owner, and he did mentioned that he replaced the odometer.  I'm assuming that he might have replace the cluster at the same time.  Everything else in the cluster is registering just fine, and they replaced the tach filter.  I just wish that GM had marked their cluster in order to know the difference between the 4 cylinder and 6 cylinder clusters.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||06-08-2000||03:26 PM||||I was nervous about taking my instrument cluster out the first time too, but after doing it a dozen times, now I can have it out in 15 minutes, easy (and I'm slow). The tach is the only thing different and you can just change one little cheap capacitor to make it work right. My Big Fiero Book lists which capacitors to use for 4, 6, or 8 cylinders. Let me know and I'll look it up for you.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-08-2000||05:45 PM||||'84s had a volt gauge under/in the tach.<P>With that out of the way, don't forget to remove/disconnect the switches.  My tach is doing the same thing.  Reads about 1500rpms faster than what it's really doing.  I pulled the tach out of the last set of gauges I bought (long story) and plan on swapping tachs (the other one works fine) sometime next week.  Be careful when you're removing and installing C3 (the connector behind the tach).  I must have pulled mine out crooked and ruined the flex plate (plastic/metal thing behind the gauges).  Be careful.  Be careful.  Since I'm mechanically challenged, I'm gonna spend the extra $$ from now on and let someone else do the work.  Would have just saved me $125 for new gauges. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
